From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OddBlueDog <3959851+OddBlueDog@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 10:31:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt7915: TWT as the standard and devices expect it

What the responder did with requests fell short of 802.11ax in ways a device
would notice, and it had no limits a network could set or anything to show:

- A TWT Teardown's octet reached the driver whole, and anything above 7 was
  ignored as no such flow. A device saying "all of them" kept its agreements
  here, and was sent to on a schedule it had stopped keeping. The octet is
  parsed: the flow ID, another negotiation type left alone, Teardown All.
- The answer to a request can be lost, and a device asks again. It got Reject.
  The same terms on the same flow now get Accept and the agreement's next wake
  time, with nothing sent to the firmware.
- The device's flow ID was replaced by the lowest free one. It's kept. New
  terms on a flow replace it, the old agreement out of the firmware first,
  where they used to make a second flow.
- A wake duration in TUs was rejected. It's converted, and the device hears
  back in the unit it asked in.
- Dictate carried no wake time. Alternate and Dictate now always do, and
  they're how limits are answered: Dictate to a Demand, Alternate otherwise.
  Nothing is agreed until the device's next request takes the terms up.
- Flows of both bands shared one schedule, though each band has its own TSF.
- A target wake time already gone, or a moment away, is moved on by whole
  intervals to one the device can still make.
- A firmware restart drops agreements, and the device went on sleeping by
  them. It's now told, once mac80211 has put the station back, with a TWT
  Teardown built here: mac80211's own sender isn't exported.

The firmware reports a station with an agreement as dozing for as long as
the agreement lasts, whatever it's doing, and sends its frames only during
its wake periods. With ps_sync the driver would then hold the frames back
from the firmware the whole time, with one per AC in it. Such a station is
left out of the hold, and when an agreement begins or ends the hold is worked
out again, as no event may be coming.

Each radio has limits, in debugfs twt_policy: the longest and shortest wake
interval, the least wake duration, agreements on the radio and per device,
and whether trigger-enabled agreements are offered (the firmware triggers in
their wake periods whether its UL OFDMA is on or not). Longest defaults to
60 s, as a device that sleeps for minutes can be dropped for not answering.
twt_status has the radio's agreements and what's happened to requests, and
twt_teardown ends one or all of a device's agreements and tells it.

KUnit: each of the above, the limits and their parsing, the teardown frame and
the status lines.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
